<onlyinclude>'''Chiplet-based integration''' partitions a system into multiple separately manufactured dies and connects them at package scale. Its feasibility depends jointly on functional partitioning, die-to-die communication, test coverage, manufacturing yield and package-level electrical and thermal constraints.</onlyinclude>

== Architectural partitioning ==
Partition boundaries determine which transactions traverse the package rather than on-die wires. The designer must budget link bandwidth, serialization, protocol overhead, latency, clock-domain crossing, coherence (where applicable), and power. Separating I/O and compute onto different process nodes can reduce the area fabricated at the most expensive node, but extra interfaces and package complexity can offset the savings.

== Interconnect and packaging ==
UCIe 1.0 specifies an industry die-to-die interface framework for standard and advanced packaging; it is not a guarantee of plug-and-play compatibility across arbitrary chiplets. The 2022 IEEE UCIe paper describes physical, channel, protocol and packaging design. The 2024 Nature Electronics work investigates a proposed 3D extension at shrinking bump pitches; its measured or modeled results should not be generalized to every package. Side-by-side organic-substrate, bridge and interposer implementations differ in routing density, reach and power. Vertical stacking adds thermal and bonding constraints.

== Yield and cost: illustrative model ==
Under an idealized independent random-defect Poisson model, die yield is <math>Y(A)=e^{-DA}</math>, where <math>D</math> is defects per unit area and <math>A</math> is die area. For an illustrative <math>D=0.005\,\mathrm{mm}^{-2}</math>, a 200 mm² monolithic die has <math>Y=e^{-1}\approx0.3679</math>. Each of four 50 mm² dies has <math>Y=e^{-0.25}\approx0.7788</math>. If four unscreened dies are assembled with independent defects, their joint survival probability is <math>(e^{-0.25})^4=e^{-1}\approx0.3679</math>: partitioning alone does not improve the probability that all four random dies are good. The economic opportunity arises from screening, selecting known-good dies, die counts per wafer, reuse and process-node choices. Real yield additionally depends on defect clustering, edge losses, assembly yield, test escape rates and correlated failures. This example is not a product forecast.

== Design and verification limits ==
Die-level functional verification does not replace link compliance, power-integrity, thermal, package-assembly and post-assembly testing. Inter-die links create new fault surfaces. Partition optimization is workload- and technology-dependent: a chiplet system may perform worse or cost more than a monolithic design.

